# CODEOWNERS — who is automatically asked to review changes to which files.
#
# HOW TO FINISH THIS FILE
# -----------------------
# Replace @polilingo/dev-two with the second developer's GitHub username, and check
# that @muhammaduzair11 is correct. Then delete this block.
#
# IMPORTANT: this file is silently ignored if the team it names does not have at least
# Write access to the repository. There is no error — reviews simply never get
# requested. Confirm at Settings -> Collaborators and teams that @polilingo/engineering
# has the Write role. (See bootstrap/02-github-setup.md step 6.)
#
# WHY THIS FILE IS SHORT
# ----------------------
# With two people, "required approvals: 1" already means the other person reviews
# everything. Listing every path here would just be that rule with extra steps. This
# file covers only the paths where the WRONG person reviewing would be a real loss —
# the ones where a mistake is expensive, silent, or hard to undo.


# Default: either developer may review anything.
*                               @polilingo/engineering


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Hot files
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# components/polilingo.tsx is 41 KB and app/globals.css is 71 KB. Both are touched by
# almost every feature, so with two people working in parallel they are where merge
# conflicts come from.
#
# The fix is not this file — it is splitting them, which is scheduled for week 1 and
# tracked as a task on the board. Until that lands, the hot-file protocol in
# docs/process/engineering-workflow.md applies: say in the daily message that you are
# going into one of these, keep the change small, and merge the same day.

/components/polilingo.tsx       @polilingo/engineering
/app/globals.css                @polilingo/engineering


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Things that are expensive to get wrong
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Learning state. `lib/progress.ts` decides XP, streaks and what is unlocked, and its
# anti-double-award logic is subtle. It is also the data that has to migrate into
# accounts without destroying anyone's streak. Changes here need a second pair of eyes
# and a test.
/lib/progress.ts                @polilingo/engineering
/tests/                         @polilingo/engineering

# Build, CI and configuration. A mistake here is either invisible or breaks everything.
/.github/                       @polilingo/engineering
/package.json                   @polilingo/engineering
/package-lock.json              @polilingo/engineering
/next.config.ts                 @polilingo/engineering
/tsconfig.json                  @polilingo/engineering
/.oxlintrc.json                 @polilingo/engineering
/.oxfmtrc.json                  @polilingo/engineering
/.gitattributes                 @polilingo/engineering

# The environment contract. Every change here is a change to what the app expects at
# runtime and to what may or may not be sent to browsers.
/.env.example                   @polilingo/engineering

# Curriculum still living in code. This file moves to the content repository during
# v0.2; until then, changes to it are content changes wearing a TypeScript costume and
# deserve the same scrutiny a content pull request would get.
/lib/courses.ts                 @polilingo/engineering

# The asset pipeline. Regenerating the image ladder incorrectly is not caught by any
# test — it just looks slightly wrong on some screens.
/scripts/                       @polilingo/engineering
/lib/art-widths.mjs             @polilingo/engineering
